            `<div style='overflow-y:scroll;height:300px;'><h3> Online Safety Bill fails to pass in Parliament, first in Singapore’s history </h3>

<p> <b> Channel News Asia </b>
<p> <b> 5 November 2025 </b>

<p> SINGAPORE: 16 Members of Parliament (MPs) from the People’s Action Party (PAP) joined opposition MPs in voting against the Online Safety Bill on Wednesday (Nov 5), marking the first time a bill has failed in Singapore’s parliament.

<p> The proposed legislation, which would have required digital platforms to disclose or gather information on perpetrators of online crime and called for the creation of an expanded scope of harms for digital offences, did not reach the simple majority needed to pass in parliament.

<p> Opposition to the bill was led by Leader of the Opposition, Pritam Singh of the Workers’ Party (WP) who rallied opposition MPs in rejecting the bill in a show of unity, leading to all Opposition MPs present in the vote rejecting the bill’s passage.

<p> Speaking to CNA, Mr Singh has lauded the failure of the bill as one of the first ‘major successes’ for the opposition. 

<p> “I think that rejecting bills that go against the interests of Singaporeans should be the main goal of the Opposition”, he said. “I’m glad that the Workers Party could stand together with the rest of the Opposition on this matter”.

<p> Other opposition figures have also welcomed the outcome. Singapore Democratic Party Secretary-General Chee Soon Juan described the bill’s failure as a ‘monumental shift in Singapore’s political landscape’ in a social media post following the vote.

<p> Within the PAP, the vote represented an unprecedented setback after decades of parliamentary dominance. PAP Party Whip Sim Ann, expressed her ‘deepest disappointment’ on the bill’s failure in a statement. The 16 PAP MPs who broke ranks during the vote are expected to face internal disciplinary action for not adhering to party discipline during the vote. However, Jalan Besar GRC MP Shawn Loh, who was among the PAP MPs to vote against the bill, said he had ‘no regrets’ about his decision.

<p> ‘I did what was right for my constituency and Singapore,’ Mr Loh said. 

<p> More updates are to come.</div>`
